private static Object copyArrayGrow1(final Object array, final Class<?> newArrayComponentType) {
    // Check if input array is null
    if (array == null) {
        // Throw an IllegalArgumentException if array is null
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input array must not be null");
    }

    // Determine the length of the array
    int length = Array.getLength(array);

    // Create a new array of the appropriate size with the default value
    Object result = newInstance(newArrayComponentType, length + 1);

    // Copy the elements from the original array to the new array
    System.arraycopy(array, 0, result, 0, length);

    // Return the new array
    return result;
}
```
In this solution, we first check if the input array is null and throw an `IllegalArgumentException` if it is. We then determine the length of the original array and create a new array of the appropriate size with the default value using the `newInstance` method. We copy the elements from the original array to the new array using `System.arraycopy`. Finally, we return the new array.

```java
public static Object copyArrayGrow1(Object array, Class<?> newArrayComponentType) {
    if (array == null) {
        final Class<?> clazz = newArrayComponentType;
        return Array.newInstance(clazz, 1);
    }

    int length = Array.getLength(array);
    Object newArray = Array.newInstance(array.getClass().getComponentType(), length + 1);
    System.arraycopy(array, 0, newArray, 0, length);

    return newArray;
}
